  "query_preview": "../Sunday through Wednesday/...The upper level ridge \nis expected to begin to dissipate Sunday as a weak trough builds \nover the gulf, shifting the region toward zonal flow that will \nbring cooler, more saturated air onshore. Temperatures are \nexpected to follow a cooling trend, due to 850mb level temps \ndropping, and leading to daily highs in the mid 60s by next week, \nthough inland and southern areas may remain warmer in the low 70s \ndue to the residual influence of a weakening surface ridge. As \nsurface high pressure also weakens, a series of weak frontal bands\noriginating from a surface trough dropping into the gulf from the\nAlaska Peninsula will move across the panhandle, resulting in \nwidespread overcast skies and moisture advection in the low to mid\nlevels. The northeast gulf coast, particularly near Yakutat, will\nbe the primary focus for precipitation, with the most significant\nactivity expected between Monday and Tuesday. While deterministic\nmodels show some spread still, the highest forecaster confidence \nresults in 24 hour rainfall accumulations of 0.75\u201d to 1.25\u201d with \nthis disturbance. The remainder of the week is expected to stay \nshowery with lighter, intermittent rain and cooler temperatures \nacross the panhandle, offering relief from the recent dry spell.",
